WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.008
SPEAKER: You've got
data and you know how

00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:03.600
to get it into Cloud Storage.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.050
Now let's talk about who you
want to have access to it.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.180
I'm in a sharing
mood, so I'll give you

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.970
permission to learn more.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.160
Today on Cloud Storage
Bytes we're talking

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.780
about controlling access--

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.750
keeping the right people in
the loop and the wrong people

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:19.900
out of it.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.700
Let's dive in.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.910
When you first create
a bucket, you'll

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.490
need to decide whether you
want to apply permissions using

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.310
uniform or fine grained access.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:39.210
In general, we recommend using
uniform bucket-level access.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.370
This method allows
you to use IAM

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.780
to manage all of
your permissions

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.270
for all of the
objects in a bucket.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.420
This might mean rethinking how
you are picturing your storage

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.620
approach.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.900
Let's imagine you
run a dog daycare

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.360
and you take photos
of the dogs every day

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.100
so their owners
can log in and see

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.050
what their pet has been up to.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.970
If you have a bucket
of photos where you're

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.190
storing all of
those, then you would

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.890
need to set the permissions
for each individual photo

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.010
so that you aren't sharing
Remy's photos with Willow's

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.060
owner.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.270
If, however, you create
a bucket for each owner,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.310
then you can grant
permissions for that bucket,

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.050
lowering the risk
of data exposure.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:20.520
Now, if Willow's owner adds a
second person to the account,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.180
one permission change will
share all of Willow's photos.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.760
That's because IAM aims
to make authentication

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.660
easier by giving you full
control and visibility.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.060
Cloud IAM provides
a unified view

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.210
into the security control
of your entire organization

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.330
across your various
Google Cloud Services.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.460
This means there's
one place to check

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.230
for granting and
reviewing permissions

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:47.010
for employees, dog owners,
visitors, other franchise

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.270
locations, and more.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.830
So that's how uniform access
works for Cloud Storage.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.450
If, however, you
know you're going

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.420
to need to manage permissions
at the object level for a given

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.530
bucket, then you can
select fine grained access.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.050
This option is mainly
for integrations

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.180
that rely on legacy
access control

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.600
systems for interoperability
with other services.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.870
In addition to choosing between
uniform and fine grained access

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.970
when creating your
bucket, you also

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.070
have options for
special controls,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.650
such as signed
URLs for accessing

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.620
an object through a link
for a set amount of time,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.470
or signed policy
documents to specify

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:29.190
what can be uploaded to a
bucket, or credential access

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.440
boundaries to restrict
the permissions that

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.340
are available to an
OAuth 2.0 access token.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.080
To learn more about
these options,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.180
be sure to check out
the documentation.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.700
And don't forget to
subscribe, give us a like,

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.980
and let us know what features
of Cloud Storage you'd

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.600
like to learn more about.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.690
Thanks for joining us for this
quick byte of Cloud Storage.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:52.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

